\relax 
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S1}{\ignorespaces Validation gate 1: the two NVML energy sources on the RTX~4060 Laptop GPU (enforced limit 115\,W). Counter values are the mean power implied by the counter's increase over each window.}}{1}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:gate1}{{S1}{1}{}{table.1}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S2}{\ignorespaces Validation gate 2: mode separation by model size (60 prompts per size). Ratio is the median of per-prompt think/no-think token ratios with a 95\% bootstrap interval. Marker ratio is no-think marker density divided by think-trace marker density.}}{1}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:gate2}{{S2}{1}{}{table.2}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S3}{\ignorespaces Carbon of the escalation tax under oracle detection, in grams of CO$_2$e per 1{,}000 queries. Total kWh is the policy's own energy for those queries and Tax kWh the energy of its tax alone; each carbon figure is Tax kWh multiplied by the grid intensity beside it. The four intensity columns are in gCO$_2$e/kWh: 50 for a grid carried by nuclear or hydro, 250 for a large renewable share, 475 for the figure usually quoted as the global average, and 700 for a grid carried by coal. They are round values spanning the range grids occupy, not measurements of a named region.}}{2}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:carbon}{{S3}{2}{}{table.3}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S4}{\ignorespaces Accuracy of each cell by family, in percent, over 400 prompts per family.}}{2}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:family-accuracy}{{S4}{2}{}{table.4}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S5}{\ignorespaces The public pool in cost order, over the 12{,}168 queries of nine datasets that all thirteen models answered. Cost is the provider's own charge, in US dollars per 1{,}000 queries. The $c^{*}$ share is the fraction of queries for which that model is the cheapest one that answers correctly, and the last row the queries no model answers. A bullet marks the four models on the cost--accuracy frontier, which are the rungs of the escalation ladder.}}{2}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:public-pool}{{S5}{2}{}{table.5}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S6}{\ignorespaces The six hosted cells: three model sizes crossed with two reasoning efforts, on the prompts of the local main subset. Output tokens include reasoning tokens, which the provider reports separately but bills as output. A cap hit is a truncated generation, counted as a failure exactly as a local cap hit is.}}{3}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:hosted-cells}{{S6}{3}{}{table.6}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S1}{\ignorespaces Cost per solved prompt (top) and solved rate (bottom) of the manual policies as the probability of accepting a wrong answer grows, at three probabilities of rejecting a right one. P3 makes a single attempt and keeps its answer, so detection errors do not move it.}}{4}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:detection}{{S1}{4}{}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S2}{\ignorespaces Extra energy per prompt, over the cost of $c^{*}(q)$, of routing one tier too low and one tier too high, with a failed answer recovered at $c_8$. Tiers are cells in order of mean energy. Error bars are 95\% bootstrap intervals.}}{5}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:asymmetry}{{S2}{5}{}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S3}{\ignorespaces The escalation tax per prompt counted in tokens, split into escalation and stickiness, under oracle detection (solid) and imperfect detection (hatched). Compare Figure~3 of the manuscript, which counts the same policies in joules and reverses the ranking of P2 and P3.}}{5}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:tax-tokens}{{S3}{5}{}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S4}{\ignorespaces Solved rate against net mean cost per prompt in tokens on the test split. Compare Figure~4 of the manuscript, which counts the same routers in joules; the language-model routers, which pay for their own prompts, fall behind here.}}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:frontier-tokens}{{S4}{6}{}{figure.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S5}{\ignorespaces The thirteen models of the public pool, the cost--accuracy frontier they define and where the escalation policies land on it. Cost is logarithmic, in US dollars per 1{,}000 queries. P3 sits on the top rung it always uses. P1 climbs the frontier and answers more queries than any single model in the pool, at a price between the two costliest rungs. The oracle answers still more for a third of what P3 costs.}}{7}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:public-frontier}{{S5}{7}{}{figure.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S6}{\ignorespaces The six hosted cells on the cost--accuracy plane, open for minimal reasoning effort and filled for high. Cost is logarithmic, in US dollars per 1{,}000 queries. The grey line is the frontier the escalation policies climb. The mode axis reads as the horizontal step from an open marker to the filled marker of the same model.}}{8}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:hosted-frontier}{{S6}{8}{}{figure.6}{}}
